{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Therefore, etc.</b> All the commentators said that this verse is figurative concerning those who die for the unity of the Name, and the meaning of 'many' is like 'great ones,' as in 'over every great one of his house' (Esther 1:8), and they are the prophets, and the 'mighty' are the patriarchs — the meaning being that they will have a portion among those who died for the unity [of the Name] together with the prophets. And we know that this is true, only it is not the reason of the pericope, and the correct interpretation in my eyes is as follows: therefore I will give Israel a portion among the many, and spoils from many nations:" "<b>and with the mighty.</b> Like 'when I go out of the city' (Exodus 9:29)." "<b>because, etc.</b> This is the reward because he poured out his soul to death. And some say it is like 'uncovered,' and the meaning is 'in public.' And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is like 'and she emptied her pitcher' (Genesis 24:20), and if they are two different roots, the proof is 'do not empty my soul' (Psalms 141:8), like the meaning of 'pour out.'" "<b>and with.</b> [Means] 'with.'" "<b>transgressors.</b> Who transgressed against HaShem." "<b>[was counted].</b> Thus Israel were reckoned." "<b>and he bore the sin of many.</b> For because of their suffering there was peace for all the nations, and the sin that they were liable to bear — Israel bore it." "<b>and for the transgressors.</b> On account of the transgressors." "<b>he intercedes.</b> With HaShem, like the meaning of 'seek the peace of the city' (Jeremiah 29:7). And so the interpretation of 'for the transgressors' is the gentile transgressors." "And now I have interpreted for you the entire pericope, and in my opinion 'behold my servant shall prosper' is what the prophet said concerning him: 'Behold, my servant, I support him' (Isaiah 42:1), and he said to me, 'You are my servant' (Isaiah 49:3), and so it is written, 'by his knowledge my righteous servant shall justify many' (Isaiah 53:11), and it is written, 'I gave my back to smiters' (Isaiah 50:6). And the sod is as I hinted in the first half of the book, and behold all the pericopes are joined one to another."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 53, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:45.837364068Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}